From: Theorem Labs
Subject: __init_tls: exit cleanly when the TLS mapping is refused

A static program whose thread-local storage does not fit musl's built-in
buffer gets it from one anonymous mmap during start-up.  Upstream does
not check the result: a refused mapping becomes a small negative pointer,
and the process faults on the first store through it.  The kernel model
that the fuzz target binaries are verified against may refuse any
mapping, so that fault is reachable undefined behaviour.

Check the result, and leave through exit_group with status 2, the status
with which the fuzz target's driver refuses an input.  A raw system call,
with no message and no a_crash: nothing that needs TLS exists at this
point.  Every build links this one libc.a, so plain and instrumented
programs leave the same way.

Applies on top of 0001 (v1.2.6-20, f21a9653).
--- a/src/env/__init_tls.c
+++ b/src/env/__init_tls.c
@@ -147,9 +147,15 @@
 			SYS_mmap2,
 			0, libc.tls_size, PROT_READ|PROT_WRITE,
 			MAP_ANONYMOUS|MAP_PRIVATE, -1, 0);
-		/* -4095...-1 cast to void * will crash on dereference anyway,
-		 * so don't bloat the init code checking for error codes and
-		 * explicitly calling a_crash(). */
+		/* Upstream leaves the result unchecked: -4095...-1 cast to
+		 * void * crashes on dereference.  Here a refused mapping is
+		 * a clean, fixed-status exit instead -- the kernel model this
+		 * libc is verified against may refuse any mapping, and a
+		 * process that faults on that refusal has no defined
+		 * behaviour to prove.  Raw syscall: nothing that needs TLS
+		 * exists yet.  Status 2 is the refusal status the drivers use. */
+		if ((unsigned long)mem > -4096UL)
+			__syscall(SYS_exit_group, 2);
 	} else {
 		mem = builtin_tls;
 	}
